A||N||4||JB||84-87vs88 differences||||1||
Z||000000||JB||11-14-2001||12:44 AM||||I understand that the 88gt had the new suspension but do the changes apply to the 88 se with 4cyl also? I have an 87 that I was going to use for a northstar swap but I can get an 88 coupe for $500.00 running!   I have $100.00 in the 87.  Is it worth the difference? I need to know quick!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||shark93726||11-14-2001||01:07 AM||||Very good price for any 88.  All the 88s have the better suspension, but the rear anti sway bar only comes on the GTs, no big deal, you can add that if you want later.<P>'88s will always be worth more in the same condition, both from scarcity, and from better suspension.  Just check that it is not severely rusted or bent frame.<P>Suspension is significantly better for a street driven Fiero, although if solid suspension bushiongs are used, ALL Fieros will be nearly equal in handling, with maybe a teeny advantage to the '88s.<P>Hope this helps ya.<P>Gerald Storvik||||reg||1
Z||000002||watts||11-14-2001||01:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by shark93726:<BR><B>but the rear anti sway bar only comes on the GTs</B> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Formula's also have the rear sway bar<BR>(want a picture of one?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  )<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <B>although if solid suspension bushiongs are used, ALL Fieros will be nearly equal in handling, with maybe a teeny advantage to the '88s.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The 88's don't have the bump steer problem that's inherint in all the previous years, and are basiclly more directionally stable than the others as well (since this setup was never meant to steer like the early ones when it was up front of the X body).  This alone would make using the '88 rear suspension worth it.  <P>If you want use the rear in your early body though, you'll have to shell out about $350us to Held motorsports for there coil over conversion and strut tower relocation kit (the towers on the '88s are more canted inward).<P>You'll also need custom brake flex lines made up as the calipers move to the rearward side of the axle.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JB||11-14-2001||09:38 PM||||Thanks guys for your quick response.  I went and cranked the 88se today and it ran but not good.  I really dont care about that as long as I have the good suspension.  Your info is very helpful.  I noticed a lot of bumpsteer in my 85 gt but I thought it was because it was worn out or something because I havent had a chance to check it out since I bought it a month ago.  That car runs good with its 2.8 but the handleing leaves a lot to be desired.  I think I'll sell it and buy the 88 and use the body panels and motor from the 88 to build my basketcase 87.   Thanks in helping me with my decision!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||11-14-2001||10:45 PM||||88 springs are way softer for a much better ride without the jars. handel way better to.<BR>88 add better brakes vented to stay cooler.<BR>88coupe only needs rear bar to be same as GT.<BR>88 are a better car to hotrod as brake and sept are up for more power with out poly bushings and harder ride they bring.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
